sec.p:
To calculate the CI, utilization reports were categorized by type of use, and the frequency of each report was recorded. The index was then determined using the following equation [23]:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:\mathrm{CI\:=\:}\sum_{\mathrm{u=}{\mathrm{u}}_{\mathrm{1}}}^{{\mathrm{u}}_{\mathrm{NC}}}\sum_{\mathrm{i=}{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{1}}}^{{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{NC}}}\frac{\mathrm{U}{\mathrm{R}}_{\mathrm{ui}}}{\mathrm{N}}$$\end{document}

The Plant Part Value (PPV) quantifies the frequency with which different plant parts are used in traditional practices, indicating which parts—such as leaves, fruits, roots, stems, or flowers—are most commonly utilized by the local community. Following Gomez-Beloz [24], PPV is calculated as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:\mathrm{PPV\:=}\:\frac{{\sum\:\mathrm{RU}}_{\mathrm{(plant\:parts)}}}{\sum\:\mathrm{RU}}{\times100}$$\end{document}

The Informant Consensus Factor (Fic) assesses the degree of agreement among informants regarding the use of medicinal plants within a specific health category. It helps identify ailments for which the community holds a strong, shared knowledge of plant-based remedies. Fic is calculated as [25]:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:{\mathrm{F}}_{\mathrm{ic}}\mathrm{\:=\:}\frac{{\mathrm{N}}_{\mathrm{ur}}\mathrm{-\:}{\mathrm{N}}_{\mathrm{t}}}{{\mathrm{N}}_{\mathrm{ur}}\mathrm{-1}}$$\end{document}

The Fidelity Level (FL) quantifies the degree to which a plant species is specifically associated with a particular medicinal use among informants. It represents the proportion of people who report using a species for the same health condition, reflecting the specificity and consistency of its traditional application. FL is calculated as [26]:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:\mathrm{FL\:=\:}\frac{{\mathrm{I}}_{\mathrm{p}}}{{\mathrm{I}}_{\mathrm{u}}}\times{100}$$\end{document}

To estimate the economic value, the mean price consumers were willing to pay for each species was calculated by averaging its maximum and minimum market prices. The annual economic value was then determined using the following equation:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ EV{\rm{ }} = {\rm{ }}AP \times SM \times SP$$\end{document}

To assess similarities in species composition among districts, the Jaccard Similarity Index (JI) was employed, a widely accepted measure for evaluating the degree of overlap between datasets. The index was calculated as follows [30]:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ JI{\rm{ }} = {\rm{ }}a/\left( {a{\rm{ }} + {\rm{ }}b{\rm{ }} + {\rm{ }}c} \right)$$\end{document}
